**Day 40: NoSQL Mini Project – Employee Directory**  

**Topic:**  
Application of MongoDB CRUD operations, query operators, and aggregation pipelines to build a functional Employee Directory. This project consolidates skills learned during Weeks 7–8, including schema design, indexing, and performance optimization.  

**Learning Objectives:**  
• Implement MongoDB CRUD operations in a real-world scenario.  
• Apply query operators ($eq, $gt, $sort) and aggregation stages ($match, $group).  
• Design a scalable schema for an employee management system.  

**Activities / Deliverables:**  
• Create an Employee Directory database with collections for employees and departments.  
• Perform CRUD operations (insert, update, delete) on employee records.  
• Build an aggregation pipeline to analyze data (e.g., average salary by department).  

**Important Concepts:**  
• **Embedding vs. Referencing:** Decide when to embed (e.g., employee skills) or reference (e.g., department details) documents.  
• **Indexing:** Use `createIndex()` to optimize queries on frequently accessed fields like `employee_id` or `department`.  

**SQL Syntax / Commands:**  
• `db.employees.insertOne({name: "John", role: "Developer"})`  
• `db.employees.aggregate([{$group: {_id: "$department", avgSalary: {$avg: "$salary"}}}])`  

**Real-world Use Cases:**  
• **HR Systems:** Track employee details, roles, and departmental hierarchies.  
• **Analytics:** Generate reports on workforce distribution or salary trends.  

**Tools / Tech:**  
• MongoDB 7  
• MongoDB Compass (for GUI-based data import/export and query building)  

**Summary:**  
The mini-project reinforced MongoDB’s flexibility for unstructured data, emphasizing schema design, performance tuning, and aggregation for actionable insights.  

